{"text":"### Logical Deduction: Stack vs. Queue\n\nTo understand the difference between a stack and a queue, we must look at how they handle the order of data processing. Both are linear data structures, but they operate on opposite principles regarding which element is processed first.\n\n#### 1. The Stack (LIFO)\nA **Stack** operates on the principle of **LIFO** (Last-In, First-Out). Imagine a stack of plates in a cafeteria or a stack of books on a table.\n*   **Mechanism:** You can only add or remove items from the \"top\" of the stack.\n*  ","output_ids":[13962,59977,48704,2567,25,13875,5972,13,18182,271,1206,3418,279,6463,1881,264,5436,321,264,6951,11,567,1902,1353,506,1204,781,3579,279,1906,314,795,8427,13,11396,513,13094,795,13976,11,694,781,14061,383,13600,15694,8559,864,2315,369,14789,1118,13,271,794,220,16,13,561,13875,318,43,25166,8,198,32,2972,4195,332,25224,383,279,16988,314,2972,43,25166,332,318,5661,30463,11,5339,81600,553,36541,264,5436,314,23702,303,264,90613,466,264,5436,314,6265,383,264,1898,13,198,9,256,2972,55157,65526,64700,1394,628,1132,884,466,3921,3470,494,279,328,3369,1,314,279,5436,13,198,9,256],"meta_info":{"id":"0c0f1266e0a04fefa6ae4b2f8467e7f2","finish_reason":{"type":"length","length":128},"prompt_tokens":29,"weight_version":"default","num_retractions":0,"input_token_logprobs":[],"output_token_logprobs":[[-0.7143884897232056,13962,null],[-0.6485113501548767,59977,null],[-0.417165607213974,48704,null],[-0.0004524161049630493,2567,null],[-0.8380770683288574,25,null],[-0.8097568154335022,13875,null],[-0.0010832638945430517,5972,null],[-0.0007415643776766956,13,null],[-0.00012861855793744326,18182,null],[-0.0006962973857298493,271,null],[-0.15895815193653107,1206,null],[-0.020820043981075287,3418,null],[-0.00021169328829273582,279,null],[-0.38401180505752563,6463,null],[-0.01769084297120571,1881,null],[-0.051217902451753616,264,null],[-0.8395110368728638,5436,null],[-0.00016664070426486433,321,null],[-0.0009124883217737079,264,null],[-9.82